= Conditional probability =

== Overview ==
'''Conditional probability''' is the probability assigned to an event when another event or body of information is taken as given.

It is written:

<pre>
P(A | B)
</pre>

read as “the probability of A given B”.

== Basic definition ==
When P(B) > 0:

<pre>
P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)
</pre>

Conditioning restricts attention to cases in which B occurs.

== Direction matters ==
Conditional probability is asymmetric in general:

<pre>
P(A | B) ≠ P(B | A)
</pre>

Confusing the two is a common reasoning error.

== Independence ==
Events A and B are independent when learning B does not change the probability of A:

<pre>
P(A | B) = P(A)
</pre>

when the conditional is defined.

Equivalent formulations use factorization:

<pre>
P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B)
</pre>

== Bayes' theorem ==
Conditional probabilities in opposite directions are related by Bayes' theorem:

<pre>
P(A | B)
= P(B | A)P(A) / P(B)
</pre>

when the denominator is nonzero.

This identity is central to [[Bayesian inference]].

== Conditioning and causation ==
Conditioning is an observational/probabilistic operation.

A causal intervention asks what would happen if a variable were actively set.

Therefore:

<pre>
P(Y | X=x)
≠ causal effect of setting X=x
</pre>

without additional assumptions.

